  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Humbug (Perfect Information 2-Player Whist). Setup: 52-card deck, 26 cards each, all face-up. Trump: Final card determines trump suit. Gameplay: 52 tricks, non-dealer leads first. Follow suit if able; else any card. Highest suit card or trump wins trick. Scoring: Win >26 tricks = win deal. Revoke (fail to follow) = lose 3 tricks. Strategic depth: Perfect information requires optimal play calculation.',
    Player: 'Welcome to Humbug, the 18th-century perfect information whist game. You and your opponent each get 26 cards, all laid out face-up before play. No hidden informationâ€”you know exactly what each player holds. The trump suit is set by the final card. Play 52 tricks, follow suit if able, and win more than 26 tricks to claim victory.',
    objective: 'Win more than 26 tricks to be the winner of the deal.',
    gameplay: '1. Non-dealer leads the first card.\n2. Standard Whist rules: Must follow suit if possible.\n3. Highest card of the suit led wins the trick, unless a trump is played.\n4. Winner of the trick leads the next.\n5. **Perfect Information**: Since all cards are dealt, players know exactly what the opponent holds at all times.',
    keyRules: [
      'Follow the legal action constraints defined for Humbug.',
      'Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: The game is scored by the number of tricks won.',
      'Winning condition: Win more than 26 tricks to be the winner of the deal.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: true,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'Exactly two players. Humbug was designed as a high-stakes two-hand whist variant for two people who wanted the rigor of Whist without partners; it is documented in London publications from the 1790s (e.g. The Sporting Magazine).',
      deck: 'Standard 52-card deck. Ranking: A (High) to 2 (Low).',
      equipment: 'Standard 52-card deck. Ranking: A (High) to 2 (Low).',
      dealing: 'Deal all 52 cards, one at a time. The final card (belonging to the dealer) is turned up to determine the Trump suit. Both players then layout their hand face-up by suit to see the complete game state.',
    },
    turnFlow: '1. Non-dealer leads the first card.\n2. Standard Whist rules: Must follow suit if possible.\n3. Highest card of the suit led wins the trick, unless a trump is played.\n4. Winner of the trick leads the next.\n5. **Perfect Information**: Since all cards are dealt, players know exactly what the opponent holds at all times.',
